  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThis forecast is no turkey, so gobble, gobble, gobble it up!\nWyoming remains on the outer edge of the longer H5 ridge along\nthe west coast, keeping temperatures held well above normal for\nlate November. There is a very weak wave embedded in the\nwesterly flow around the ridge, but that will only succeed in\nbringing more dense high clouds across the state today. Highs\ntoday will be well into the 40s, with low 50s along the I-25\ncorridor. \n\nThe ridge gets shunted to the west by the next weather system,\nwith will have more impact on western Wyoming starting late\nFriday afternoon. With winds aloft shifting more from the\nnorthwest, this will be a more favorable setup for snowfall for\nthe western mountains. Snowfall forecast has held steady Friday\nand Saturday, so Winter Storm Watches have been hoisted for the\nwestern mountains starting Friday morning, while the Bighorns\nstart Friday evening. Current likely snowfall amounts for the \nwestern mountains currently holding in the 10 to 14 inch range, \nwith lighter amounts for Jackson Hole/Star Valley/Upper Green \nRiver. Snowfall will continue through Friday night into Saturday\nmorning, and colder temperatures in remaining lower elevations \nwill allow some light snowfall for most of the area by Saturday \nmorning. Northwest flow will also bring colder temperatures back\nto the region, with highs struggling to reach the 30s both days\nthis weekend. Some of these colder temperatures will depend on \nif any snowfall accumulates in the lower elevations.\n\nIt doesn't currently look like the low elevation snows will\nremain, as southwest surface winds return promptly on Monday,\njumping temperatures back up into the 40s.",
